 **Given:** The graph coefficient $U_n$ and the rooted majorant condition [A1].
 
-1.1 Fix $n\geq2$ and a tuple $(\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_n)$, and let $H$ be the simple graph on $[n]$ whose edge $\{i,j\}$ is present exactly when $\gamma_i\sim\gamma_j$. By [F1], $U_n=\sum_{G\subseteq H,\ G\text{ connected}}(-1)^{|E(G)|}$. Here the complete graph on $[n]$ has edge set $\binom{[n]}2$, and $G\subseteq H$ means that $G$ has the same vertex set and $E(G)\subseteq E(H)$. Represent each edge $\{i,j\}$ with $i<j$ by $(i,j)$ and order these pairs lexicographically, first by $i$ and then by $j$; list the edges as $e_1<_{\rm lex}\cdots<_{\rm lex}e_m$ and assign rank $\rho(e_k)=k\cdot1_{\mathbb R}$. For a tree $T$, its rank sum is $\sum_{e\in E(T)}\rho(e)$. By [F8], each connected graph $G$ has a spanning tree; by [F9], its spanning trees form a finite nonempty set, so their finite real rank sums have a minimum. A tree has a unique path between any two vertices: connectedness gives a path, and two distinct paths would contain a cycle between their first divergence and next common vertex, contradicting acyclicity. Removing an edge from a tree leaves exactly two connected components; call their vertex partition the cut determined by that edge. The endpoints lie on opposite sides, since a path between them avoiding the removed edge would create a cycle, and every path between the endpoints in any graph omitting that edge must cross the cut. The minimum-rank-sum spanning tree is unique: if distinct minimizing trees $T,S$ existed, take the least-ranked edge $e$ in their symmetric difference and, after swapping their names if needed, take $e\in T\setminus S$. Deleting $e$ from $T$ gives a cut crossed by the $S$-path between the endpoints of $e$; a crossing edge $f$ on that path lies in $S\setminus T$, and replacing $f$ by the earlier edge $e$ lowers the rank sum of $S$ by [F10], a contradiction. For a tree $T$, let $A(T)\subseteq\binom{[n]}2\setminus E(T)$ consist of the edges later than every edge on their unique $T$-path. If $G$ contains $T$ and a non-tree edge $e$ is earlier than a path edge $f$, then $T-f+e$ is a spanning tree of lower rank sum by [F10], so $T$ is not minimum. Conversely, if every non-tree edge of $G$ is later than every edge on its $T$-path, then for each $f\in T$ every other edge of $G$ crossing the cut made by deleting $f$ is later than $f$. If a competing spanning tree $S$ omitted $f$, the $S$-path between the endpoints of $f$ crosses this cut at an edge $e$; adding $f$ to $S$ and deleting that path edge $e$ gives a spanning tree with lower rank sum by [F10]. Thus $T$ is the unique minimum tree of $G$ exactly when $T\subseteq G\subseteq T\cup A(T)$. Consequently, connected subgraphs of $H$ split into disjoint classes indexed by trees $T\subseteq H$, each class consisting of $T\cup A$ for $A\subseteq A(T)\cap E(H)$. The signed sum in one class is $(-1)^{n-1}(1-1)^{|A(T)\cap E(H)|}$, whose real absolute value is at most $1$. Applying the finite-sum triangle inequality from [F5, F7] to the class contributions gives $|U_n(\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_n)|\leq\sum_{T\in\mathcal T_n}\prod_{\{i,j\}\in E(T)}\mathbf 1_{\{\gamma_i\sim\gamma_j\}}$. For $n=1$ both sides equal $1$. [F1, F5, F7, F8, F9, F10, algebra]
